  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Typical late November temperatures will continue \nthrough the end of the week. A widespread, soaking rainfall will \narrive later Thanksgiving Day, with unsettled weather then lasting \nthrough the rest of the week.\n\nStrong high pressure will drift east across the eastern Great Lakes \nand New England Wednesday through early Thursday, bringing a period \nof dry weather and seasonable temperatures. Dry weather will last \nthrough Thursday morning, then a period of unsettled weather will\nreturn.\n\nThe merger of northern and southern stream waves deepens \nsignificantly by Thursday over the Tennessee Valley as broad \nsurface low pressure forms off the Carolina coast. This low \ntracks up the east coast into Friday with a trough extending \nwestward into the Ohio Valley and Great Lakes. There is high \nconfidence that widespread precipitation will spread northward \ninto the region on Thursday as the deformation region works \nthrough with an easterly feed of moisture via the system's warm \nconveyor. Some uncertainty exists at this point concerning onset\ntime of the precipitation given dry air in place that will take\ntime to saturate, but a widespread steady soaking rain is likely\nby late in the day Thursday and into Friday. Temperatures aloft\nremain quite mild with profiles suggesting almost all rain as \nthe primary precipitation type through Friday morning, although \na few wet snowflakes or sleet pellets may mix in across higher \nterrain at times. \n\nFriday through Saturday, occasional showers will continue as the \ndeep mid level closed low crosses the region. The airmass will grow \ncold enough for some lake enhancement by Friday and Saturday, with\nearly indications pointing towards westerly flow during this \ntime frame. Late Friday through Saturday, modest cold advection \nwill drop 850 MB temperatures down to -2C to -3C. Profiles \nsuggest rain for lower elevations, but some wet snow may start \nto mix in across higher terrain. Marginal temperatures at the \nsurface and aloft suggest there will be little, if any snow \naccumulation during this period, even across higher terrain.\n\nThis system will bring beneficial rain to the region, with rainfall \namounts likely reaching 1.0\" to 1.5\" across much of the region late \nThursday through Saturday. Much of the area is now in D1 moderate \ndrought after the exceptionally dry conditions from September \nthrough today.",
